Neuroscience finds that visual imagery involves a distributed network extending from frontal control systems to sensory cortices and overlapping with default-mode regions. Fine-grained visual detail appears to recruit early visual cortex more strongly, while more abstract or semantic imagery can depend less on it.

But one can imagine without a vivid internal picture.

Someone can imagine:

A society in which nobody owns property.

without picturing every member of that society.

