11. The Pratyabhijñā insight

The Śaiva insight is not merely that consciousness represents an absent object.

It is that consciousness can manifest a determinate form without being passively forced by a currently present external source.

That is phenomenologically obvious:

I can now imagine a blue mountain.

The controversial inference is:

internally initiated manifestation⇒consciousness is absolutely free.

Science grants:

endogenous construction.

It does not grant:

uncaused construction.

The strongest synthesis is:

svatantra-vikalpa=historically conditioned but presently self-initiated
	​

generation of determinate counterfactual form.
	​


That is genuine operational autonomy without absolute causal independence.

VII. Imagination’s epistemology
12. Can imagination produce knowledge?

Yes, but only when constrained.

Imagination can reveal:

logical inconsistency;
causal consequences;
structural possibility;
practical obstacles;
another perspective;
consequences of a model;
possible actions.

Scientific thought experiments and models routinely use imagination, but their epistemic force does not come from vividness alone. It comes from constraints supplied by accepted premises, mathematics, empirical knowledge, and inferential rules. Contemporary epistemology distinguishes instructive imagination, which works within reliable constraints, from unconstrained or transcendent imagination, which merely opens possibilities.

