not:

this entity independently exists outside the psyche.
IX. Steiner: two different theories of imagination

Steiner’s thought must be divided sharply into:

his early philosophical epistemology;
his later claims about supersensible cognition.

Collapsing them produces confusion.

X. Early Steiner: imagination and freedom
20. Percept and concept

In The Philosophy of Freedom, Steiner argues that ordinary cognition unites:

percept+concept.

The percept is given; thinking supplies the intelligible relation through which the object is understood. He calls the conscious apprehension of conceptual content “intuition,” using the word differently from vague instinct or hunch.

His epistemic model is:

Knowledge(x)=Percept(x)+Concept(x).
	​


This resembles neither crude empiricism nor subjective idealism.

Steiner argues that thinking is not merely a private screen between mind and world. It is the activity through which the relation and intelligibility of the percept are grasped.

21. Moral intuition, moral imagination, moral technique

Steiner’s most technically valuable account of imagination appears in his theory of free action.

He distinguishes:

Moral intuition

The individual apprehends a guiding ethical idea.

Moral imagination

The individual generates a concrete action-form through which that idea could be realized in a unique situation.

Moral technique

The individual understands the causal and practical structure of the world well enough to implement the imagined action.

So:

intuition:
imagination:
technique:
	​

what ought I express?
what novel action could express it here?
how can that action actually work?
	​

	​


Steiner explicitly describes moral imagination as generating new moral ideas and moral technique as the ability to realize them in a determinate perceptual world.

