Steiner attempts to discipline cognition through concentration, ethical training, consistency, and repeated observation. His methodology aspires to intersubjectivity. But it lacks the level of blinded, independent, adversarial testing expected in modern empirical science.

28. Steiner’s internal safeguards

Steiner does make distinctions that prevent a completely naïve reading:

ordinary fantasy is not higher Imagination;
an image is not its interpretation;
interpretation is not identity with the being;
ethical and attentional discipline matter;
unconsciousness or uncontrolled fantasy is not cognition;
content must remain clear and consciously sustained.

His writings explicitly warn that the technical meanings of Inspiration and Intuition differ from everyday usage.

These distinctions improve the phenomenology.

They do not independently validate the ontology.

