XIV. The most rigorous epistemology of imagination

A disciplined framework must distinguish four levels.

Level 1 — Phenomenological validity

Did the experience actually occur as reported?

PhenValid(x).

Questions:

Was the image vivid?
Did it feel autonomous?
Was it stable?
Did it have affective force?
Did it transform over time?

This level requires careful description but makes no external claim.

Level 2 — Psychological validity

Does the imaginative content reveal or alter the person’s organization?

PsychValid(x).

Evidence includes:

recurrence across contexts;
relation to known conflicts;
changes in behaviour;
integration of previously dissociated material;
predictive relevance to the person’s reactions.

This is the strongest ordinary Jungian level.

Level 3 — Model validity

Does the image support a successful model of a process beyond immediate introspection?

ModelValid(x).

Tests include:

explanatory power;
novel prediction;
constraint satisfaction;
intersubjective agreement;
comparison with rival models.

Goethean imagination is strongest here when empirically constrained.

Level 4 — Ontological validity

Does the content correspond to an independently existing entity or domain?

OntValid(x).

This requires evidence not generated solely by the imaginative process itself.

Possible standards include:

independently verified information unavailable to the subject;
reproducible cross-observer structure under controlled conditions;
predictive success beyond chance;
exclusion of suggestion and prior knowledge;
robustness under adversarial replication.

Neither vividness nor psychological benefit is enough.

