XIX. What science has solved

Science strongly supports that imagination:

is distributed across control, memory, default-mode, sensory, and source-monitoring systems;
can be imagistic or non-imagistic;
overlaps with but differs from perception;
shares constructive machinery with episodic memory and future simulation;
creates counterfactual action spaces;
is historically conditioned but can generate novel configurations;
can affect perception, emotion, learning, and action;
is ordinarily distinguished from reality through source-monitoring and precision control.
XX. What remains open

Science has not established:

why generated content becomes phenomenally present;
why some imagery is conscious and other generative processing is not;
why inner figures can possess such powerful experienced autonomy;
whether symbolic imagery reveals universal psychological structures or culturally learned priors;
whether trained imagination can provide knowledge of realities inaccessible to ordinary observation;
whether genuinely novel imagination is reducible to recombination;
whether imagination is a partial expression of consciousness’s intrinsic freedom.
